WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.760
It’s hard to confuse a Jedi&nbsp;
Master with a bounty hunter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.840
but in 1980 the distinction wasn’t&nbsp;
as clear. Let’s go back in time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.680
see what it was like to watch “The Empire&nbsp;
Strikes Back” when it was first released!

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.080
While "The Empire Strikes Back" is&nbsp;
the middle chapter of a trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.360
many fans initially felt it&nbsp;
left too much to interpretation.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.400
In a letter to the science fiction film magazine&nbsp;
Starlog, one fan named Sean Bernard wrote,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.160
"I know they wanted to leave something to settle&nbsp;
in the other sequels, but they left a little too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.480
much. For instance, Han Solo's predicament.&nbsp;
The movie should not have ended until Han was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.600
either killed by Boba Fett or Jabba or rescued&nbsp;
by Lando Calrissian or Chewbacca, the former,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.840
preferably. Also, the fate of Bespin is&nbsp;
not told. Was it taken by Lando's troops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.880
taken by Imperial troops or destroyed by Vader?"

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.200
This criticism proved common enough that&nbsp;
film star Harrison Ford responded to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.680
In an interview with Rolling Stone, Ford said,

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.080
"One of [series creator] George [Lucas]'s real&nbsp;
strengths is not giving you all the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.480
you need, yet at the same time not denying&nbsp;
you anything essential. You have a feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.080
that you want to know more at all times. I&nbsp;
have heard frequently that there is a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.680
kind of disappointment with the ending of&nbsp;
the second film. I've heard people say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.960
'There's no end to this film' or 'I&nbsp;
can't wait to find out what happens.'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.240
But they will, and that's exactly&nbsp;
the effect intended by the ending."

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.960
"I am your father"

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.120
This revelation from "The Empire Strikes Back" is&nbsp;
often considered one of the most shocking moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:28.880
in movie history. The twist was so surprising,&nbsp;
in fact, that many fans in 1980 doubted what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.040
they'd just heard. Starlog reader Robert L.&nbsp;
Beedy-Scarola [BEE-dee scar-OH-la?] wrote,

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.760
"Is Luke related to Vader? Most think so now&nbsp;
that Vader came right out and said it. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.720
I say, do you believe everything you hear? Vader&nbsp;
may have lied just to enlist Luke to his side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.440
Vader would then dispose of Luke&nbsp;
once he got what he wanted."

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.200
Even most of the cast and crew were kept&nbsp;
in the dark. As Mark Hamill explained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.240
on “The Graham Norton Show” in 2017, the&nbsp;
version they filmed had an alternate twist.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.680
"In that scene, the way we filmed it,&nbsp;
Vader said, "You don't know the truth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.840
Obi-Wan killed your father."

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.080
After filming the scene, Hamill said&nbsp;
that the director, Irvin Kershner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.240
pulled him aside and told him that Darth&nbsp;
Vader is actually Luke's father. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.200
he was forbidden to tell anyone else, including&nbsp;
his castmates. That meant that even Harrison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.840
Ford and Carrie Fisher didn't learn the real line&nbsp;
until they finally saw a screening of the film.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.800
"At the screening, when that happened,&nbsp;
Harrison turned to me and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.240
"Hey kid, you didn't f—ing tell me that!"

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.520
Another line that left fans scratching their heads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.840
after the original screenings of "The Empire&nbsp;
Strikes Back" was something Yoda said to Ben.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.560
"That boy is our last hope."

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.880
"No, there is another."

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.080
One Starlog reader named Bill Smith theorized,

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:47.600
"Could it be Vader himself? Considering&nbsp;
that there was an equilibrium of power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.360
between the good and dark sides of the&nbsp;
Force, it would not be impossible to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.480
Vader into the antithesis of what he is now,&nbsp;
especially if Luke [...] is truly his son."

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.280
On the other hand, a fan&nbsp;
named Keith Hoffman wrote,

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.440
"I suggest Princess Leia. She is young enough&nbsp;
for the training; she withstood Darth Vader's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.960
tortures; she is dedicated to the cause; Princess&nbsp;
Leia, not Lando, 'heard' Luke's cries for help;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.760
Han Solo is not in shape to be going anywhere&nbsp;
for a while and he is too old. I wouldn't be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.000
surprised if in the third film, Leia,&nbsp;
instead of Luke, destroys the Emperor."

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.640
As it turns out, each of&nbsp;
them was a little bit right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.280
although not in the ways they expected. Vader did&nbsp;
switch sides at the end of "Return of the Jedi,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.000
and Leia ultimately proved to&nbsp;
be Force-sensitive as well.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.440
"I know" is often considered one of the most&nbsp;
clever improvised lines in movie history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.920
but not everyone felt that way in 1980.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.160
Starlog reader Jeanette Vogelpohl wrote,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.240
". . . Somebody should tell Harrison Ford that&nbsp;
when a woman passionately tells a man, 'I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.920
you,' 'I know' is not an acceptable response.&nbsp;
That scene was not funny, it was infuriating."

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.520
Another person who was not a fan of Ford's line&nbsp;
was George Lucas. In a 2011 interview with Jon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.720
Favreau, the Han Solo actor shared how the "Star&nbsp;
Wars" creator responded to his improvised line.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.560
"And George, I think this is fair&nbsp;
enough to say, he went apes–t.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.960
He thought it was horrible and&nbsp;
that it would get a bad laugh."

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.400
However, despite his distaste for Ford's version&nbsp;
of the scene, Lucas allowed the line to stay in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.040
for test screenings, where it was received&nbsp;
favorably by audiences. Given how iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.960
that line is today, it seems like Lucas made&nbsp;
the right call to let it remain in the film.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.000
While "The Empire Strikes Back" wasn't&nbsp;
universally beloved upon its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.920
plenty of fans recognized it as a&nbsp;
masterpiece as soon as they saw it.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.400
One positive reaction came from Starlog&nbsp;
reader David K. Abernathy, who wrote,

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.240
"After seeing [The Empire Strikes Back],&nbsp;
I came away with a feeling that finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.480
after all these years, science fiction has&nbsp;
been brought to the screen in all its glory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.120
[The Empire Strikes Back] is so rich, so full&nbsp;
of mystery and excitement and, most of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.600
wonder. [...] To me, [The Empire Strikes Back]&nbsp;
represents the first true science-fiction film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.160
with all of its parts complete: the characters,&nbsp;
the theme, the special effects and the magic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.440
It has the right to be called&nbsp;
the first science-fiction epic"

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.400
Another Starlog reader, Arlene Bahrenburg, wrote,

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.080
"I didn't think they could do it, but they&nbsp;
did. They made a movie that was better than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.440
the first Star Wars. More action,&nbsp;
more dimension to the characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.840
more and better effects — everything that&nbsp;
made Star Wars so great the first time, plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.400
much more. The Force was certainly with George&nbsp;
Lucas when he created this fabulous sequel."

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.760
David Gerrold, a science-fiction author and&nbsp;
“Star Trek” television writer, reviewed “The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:29.840
Empire Strikes Back” for Starlog #38 but was not&nbsp;
very kind to the film. In his review, he wrote,

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.600
"Just about every other critic in the country&nbsp;
has been telling you how good the picture is;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.760
they've been falling all over&nbsp;
themselves to tell you. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.600
embarrassing. I feel guilty for not&nbsp;
liking it as much as I'm supposed to."

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.280
Considering that “Star Trek” relies more&nbsp;
on grounded science than “Star Wars,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.640
Gerrold's criticisms of “The Empire&nbsp;
Strikes Back” were mostly about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.640
it disregarded real-world logistics. He&nbsp;
was frustrated by the many ways in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.000
its plot and characters seemed to ignore&nbsp;
the physics of the world they were in.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:06:01.920
"Let's go through this shower of asteroids!&nbsp;
Who cares, and, my hair won't get messed up!"

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.480
Gerrold justified his criticism by reasoning&nbsp;
that the film was using science-fiction devices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.360
even if its plot skewed closer to fantasy. He&nbsp;
also criticized Luke's growth but enjoyed other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.240
parts of the movie, including the musical score,&nbsp;
performances, production design, and effects.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.240
Many Starlog readers were&nbsp;
unimpressed with Gerrold's review.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.640
They struck back hard in Issue #40, where&nbsp;
a reader named Michael Mallory wrote,

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:28.800
"Mr. Gerrold complains about what he deems&nbsp;
storytelling flaws in logic or science in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.000
[The Empire Strikes Back]. As for the scientific&nbsp;
misrepresentations, I can only say they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.480
matter. This is a fantasy movie. [...] I'm tired&nbsp;
of people faulting these films for not being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.960
intellectual enough. Who said they were supposed&nbsp;
to be thought-provoking? Few films are perfect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.280
Nitpicking [...] is like criticizing an apple&nbsp;
for not being steak when you're hungry for steak.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.360
It might be the best apple you ever&nbsp;
tasted, but it's still lousy steak."

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.920
Some letters to Gerrold were nicer, but overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:56.560
they weren't the biggest fans&nbsp;
of Starlog's official review.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.320
Like Gerrold, professional critics were also&nbsp;
initially mixed on "The Empire Strikes Back."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:07.360
And some of them were downright savage about&nbsp;
it. Washington Post critic Judith Martin wrote,

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:11.280
"’The Empire Strikes Back' has no plot&nbsp;
structure, no character studies let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:16.320
alone character development, no emotional or&nbsp;
philosophical point to make. It has no original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.960
vision of the future, which is depicted as&nbsp;
a pastiche of other junk-culture formulae,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.640
such as the western, the costume&nbsp;
epic and the World War II movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.960
Its specialty is 'special effects' or&nbsp;
visual tricks, some of which are playful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.360
imaginative and impressive, but others of&nbsp;
which have become space-movie clichés."

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.760
Frederick S. Clarke of Cinefantastique&nbsp;
magazine was also not a fan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.640
Making a prediction that really&nbsp;
didn't age well, Clarke wrote,

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:45.280
"The Empire Strikes Back is a lifeless copy of&nbsp;
Star Wars propelled chiefly on the momentum of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.200
that earlier film. Without the likes of a&nbsp;
Peter Cushing or Alec Guinness to add some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.600
dignity and solid support, Mark Hamill,&nbsp;
Carrie Fisher and Harrison Ford flounder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:57.440
in roles that are certain to doom their&nbsp;
careers regardless of the series' success."

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:01.680
However, as with the fans, there were some&nbsp;
critics who loved "The Empire Strikes Back"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.480
right out of the gate. The legendary Gene&nbsp;
Siskel of the Chicago Tribune gave the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.480
three-and-a-half out of four stars,&nbsp;
saying his only issues with it were,

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.560
"trivial compared to the strengths of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.520
which are considerable and&nbsp;
sometimes even majestic."

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:21.120
"The Empire Strikes Back" was the cinematic&nbsp;
introduction of two characters who would go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:27.120
to be immortalized in merchandise for decades to&nbsp;
come: Yoda and Boba Fett. However, Arthur Knight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:31.120
a critic for The Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;
confused them in his review of the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:35.440
Knight's column started out smoothly, praising&nbsp;
"The Empire Strikes Back" as the best science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.840
fiction movie since "Star Wars." He also said&nbsp;
that Kershner's direction made the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:45.120
come alive even more than in the previous&nbsp;
film. However, two paragraphs in, Knight wrote,

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.600
"As to the present episode, despite a couple of&nbsp;
phoned in messages from Ben Kenobi [...] which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:54.160
collected, must last all of 40 seconds, most&nbsp;
of Luke Skywalker's good advice comes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.320
a fascinating green-hued character named Boba&nbsp;
Fett [...] — and I'm still not certain whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:02.240
he's a skillfully animated puppet or&nbsp;
a real live human being. Whichever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:06.160
he's one of Lucas' strongest assets&nbsp;
in this Star Wars incarnation."

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:11.200
Today, the archived version of the review begins&nbsp;
with an update correcting the error, but it serves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:16.720
as an amusing reminder that the icons of the&nbsp;
present were once still new and novel to fans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:21.520
However, even if he mixed up their names, Knight&nbsp;
was correct in his assumption that both Boba Fett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.560
and Yoda would go on to count themselves among&nbsp;
Lucas' strongest — and most marketable — assets.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:32.080
If some of the pushback against "The Empire&nbsp;
Strikes Back" feels strangely familiar — even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.720
if you weren't around in 1980 — there's a&nbsp;
reason for that. The initial response to "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:42.800
Empire Strikes Back" is eerily similar to that of&nbsp;
2017's "Star Wars: Episode VIII – The Last Jedi."

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:45.760
Both middle chapters of their respective trilogies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.040
tried to take the “Star Wars”&nbsp;
franchise in exciting new directions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:53.280
and both received mixed reviews from fans who&nbsp;
were initially displeased with the wide gap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:58.400
between their expectations and the film itself.&nbsp;
Thanks to the internet and social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:04.080
the backlash against “The Last Jedi” was much&nbsp;
louder, but the parallels are still unmistakable.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.400
Many fans slammed "The Last Jedi" for its&nbsp;
diversity and assumed political correctness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:12.960
in addition to critiquing the coherence of&nbsp;
its plot. Of course, few fans in 1980 were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.320
complaining about "The Empire Strikes&nbsp;
Back" being too politically correct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:20.080
but there were similar complaints about&nbsp;
the story not being coherent or compelling.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:26.880
Mike Ryan of Uproxx mentioned in a 2019 interview&nbsp;
with "The Last Jedi" writer and director Rian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:31.440
Johnson that "The Empire Strikes Back" also&nbsp;
divided fans when it was first released.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.160
The filmmaker admitted that even he himself was&nbsp;
initially disappointed with "Empire," which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:41.120
actually the first Star Wars movie he remembered&nbsp;
seeing. However, he eventually came to his senses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:46.000
saying that after watching and loving "Return&nbsp;
of the Jedi," he wound up warming to "Empire."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.720
In fact, although he wasn't initially a fan of&nbsp;
"The Empire Strikes Back," it gradually became his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:55.840
favorite of the trilogy. So perhaps if "The Last&nbsp;
Jedi" follows in the footsteps of its predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:00.320
it may someday be looked upon as&nbsp;
fondly as "The Empire Strikes Back."

